  "content": {
    "title": "Auburn basketball misses March Madness, won't play in 2026 NCAA Tournament",
    "text": "Updated March 15, 2026, 5:42 p.m. CTAUBURN — Auburn basketball won't return to the NCAA Tournament in its first season under head coach Steven Pearl, as the program learned Sunday, March 15, it didn't make the 2026 March Madness field.The Tigers were the second team out of the field, according to the CBS Selection Sunday show, behind Oklahoma, which beat it in February.The program has yet to announce whether or not it'd accept an opportunity to play in the NIT, but as it stands, Auburn's season sits at 17-16 overall and 7-11 against the SEC. Both were the Tigers' worst marks since the 2020-21 campaign, when they were barred from the postseason with a self-imposed ban.Pearl was hired in a hurry, as the program announced his father, Bruce Pearl, was retiring Sept. 22, the same day Auburn began practices for the 2025-26 season. Year One of the Steven Pearl era was, in several respects, a letdown, and it reaches a point of a major failure with Auburn's absence from March Madness.Auburn entered Selection Sunday on the NCAA Tournament bubble. Had it secured an at-large bid, it would've become the first team to land an at-large bid with more than 15 losses in the tournament's history. It played one of the toughest schedules in the nation, as well as 17 Quad 1 games. However, it scuffled to the finish line, losing seven of its final 10 games heading into the tournament.Adam Cole is the Auburn athletics beat writer for the Montgomery Advertiser. He can be reached via email atacole@gannett.com or on X, the platform formerly known as Twitter,@colereporter. To support Adam's work, pleasesubscribe to the Montgomery Advertiser.Our team of savvy editors independently handpicks all recommendations. If you purchase through our links, the USA TODAY Network may earn a commission. Prices were accurate at the time of publication but may change.",
